<details open>
    <summary>What does this site do?</summary>
    <p>
        Econ People is a search engine for economists rather than papers. You describe a research
        topic, or paste an abstract, and it finds researchers whose published work matches.
        Under the hood it runs a semantic search over a large database of economics papers and
        rolls the matching papers up to their authors. The papers that drove each match are shown
        as evidence on every author card, so you can verify why someone appears in the results.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Where does the data come from?</summary>
    <p>The site combines three public sources:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>RePEc Author Service</strong>: the registry where roughly 84,000 economists
            maintain their own profiles, covering name, current affiliation, homepage and the
            list of works they claim as theirs. This is the backbone of every author profile here.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>RePEc paper metadata</strong>: the same curated corpus of about 250 major
            journals and working paper series that powers the
            <a href="https://econpapers.eduard-bruell.de">semantic paper search</a>. It supplies
            abstracts, embeddings and citation data for the in-corpus papers of each author.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Wikidata</strong>: optional profile extras such as a photo, education,
            doctoral advisors and students, awards, and links to ORCID or Google Scholar. These
            are only available for the subset of authors whose Wikidata entry is linked to their
            RePEc author ID.
        </li>
    </ul>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Why is an affiliation outdated or missing?</summary>
    <p>
        Affiliations are taken exclusively from the author's own RePEc Author Service
        registration, never from Wikidata or any other source. If an affiliation looks out of
        date, the author can update it themselves at
        <a href="https://authors.repec.org">authors.repec.org</a>, and the change will appear
        here after the next data update.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Why doesn't a particular economist show up?</summary>
    <p>There are two common reasons:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The person is not registered with the RePEc Author Service. Only registered authors
            are included; there is no automatic name matching for unregistered authors.
        </li>
        <li>
            Their work falls outside the curated journal subset. The search can only match
            against papers that are in the database, so authors publishing mainly in outlets not
            covered here will rank low or not appear.
        </li>
    </ul>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>How does the ranking work?</summary>
    <p>
        The search first finds the papers that best match your query, then aggregates them by
        author. Three ranking modes control the aggregation:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Most papers</strong>: favours authors with many matching papers, good for finding people who work broadly in an area.</li>
        <li><strong>Best match</strong>: favours authors of the single closest papers, good for very specific topics.</li>
        <li><strong>Balanced</strong>: a blend of both.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The optional <strong>citation boost</strong> additionally weighs in how widely cited an
        author's matching work is.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>A photo or biographical detail is wrong. Where does it come from?</summary>
    <p>
        Photos, education, academic genealogy and awards come from Wikidata. If something is
        wrong, the cleanest fix is to correct the underlying Wikidata entry, which benefits every
        site that uses it. You can also email
        <a href="mailto:eduard.bruell@zew.de">eduard.bruell@zew.de</a> and I will look into it.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Does the site store anything when I search?</summary>
    <p>
        Saved searches are stored so shared links keep working.
        For maintenance and performance monitoring the backend keeps lightweight statistical logs:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>IP address</li>
        <li>A hash of the query text (not the text itself)</li>
        <li>Number of authors returned</li>
        <li>Top three author identifiers</li>
        <li>Ranking mode and filter flags used</li>
        <li>Response time</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        These entries support debugging and quality improvements. They are not shared with third parties.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Who can I contact if something looks wrong?</summary>
    <p>
        Please send corrections, feedback or unexpected results to
        <a href="mailto:eduard.bruell@zew.de">eduard.bruell@zew.de</a>.
    </p>
</details>


